# PURGATORY: the game's second mode. You are SCARF, alone in a pitch-black place built for things
# with no soul, and you have one. The dark gives way to brick as you walk; somewhere in it lies
# THE GUN. Past it, one level for every fighter of the Arena, each built from that fighter's own
# place, full of the Purgatory's small dead (zombies, skeletons, wraiths), and at its end the
# fighter themself, stronger than in the Arena.
#
# Scarf has four menus of four moves, picked with Z / X / C / V and fired with 1-4: STANCE (Z: physical, costs stamina, learned at
# dojos), ART (X: magic, costs mana, learned from cursed books), ARMAMENT (C: the weapon's own
# skills, chosen in the inventory) and REFLEX (V: the focus moves). The letter of the open menu
# closes it again. R is the weapon's basic attack, F blocks, Q dashes, E opens the inventory,
# WASD walks and the mouse or the arrow keys turn the camera. F6 opens the admin panel (cheats;
# turning it on switches achievements off until a new run).
#
# Out of combat the inventory and the pause menu stop time. Once an enemy notices you the camera
# locks on and the fight music starts; once a blow is traded you are locked in combat, and the
# world no longer waits for you.
#
# playerversus.fw drives it: open(), then update() every tick, draw_scene() inside the 3D pass,
# after_scene() while that camera is still set, and draw_hud() on the 640x360 HUD.
#
# This file is not part of the game's own package: it comes in the optional playerversus_purgatory
# package with the mode's music. Before building, the game's loader (loader.fw) copies it into the
# game's folder as purgatory_mode.fw (it imports roster.fw, puppet.fw and save.fw from there); with
# the package missing it copies purgatory_stub.fw instead, which has the same interface and no mode.
import aperture as ap
import math
